Replay write-back is sorted, deduplicated and coalesced

The other half of the recovery hang: every replayed block went through the
block cache and was flushed on release, one device command per block, in
log order -- random order, as the medium sees it. Worse than the command
count is the multiplier: a hot metadata block logged in two hundred
transactions was written two hundred times, each copy overwritten by the
next moments later. The incident sample had 43% of replay wall-time inside
ext4_bcache_free -> ext4_block_flush_buf.

Collect replayed blocks in a 4 MiB batch instead. At flush, sort by target
block with arrival order as the tie-break, keep only the newest copy of
each block -- the state sequential replay would have ended with -- and
write each physically-contiguous run with a single device command. The
batch is also the memory bound: it flushes when full, so a journal of any
size replays in 4 MiB installments.

Like the read window, the batch bypasses the block cache: during recovery
nothing else is writing. The one exception is a block already resident --
the journal inode's own metadata, group descriptors read while opening the
journal -- where a stale cached copy released dirty would later be flushed
over the replayed data. Those residents are rewritten in place and marked
clean before the direct write lands.

Errors change shape too, for the better. The old path lost write errors
entirely: the tag callback returns void and the flush inside the cache
release swallowed its result, so recovery would clear a journal whose
contents never reached the medium. Batched flushes report; a failed flush
now fails jbd_recover(), EXT4_FINCOM_RECOVER stays set, and the next mount
tries again.

Measured on the incident's shape: replay writes fall from 29k device
commands (120 MB) to 2k (29 MB).

